the average u.s household consumes about  11 000 kilowatt hours of electricity a  year  the biggest share of that power about 16  goes to this  air conditioning summer months bring  higher temperatures and higher  electricity bills but a wall street  journal analysis found you could be  paying more than your neighbors to keep  your house cool  and may not even know it even if the  power is generated in the same exact  place  and transmitted on the same exact power  [Music]  lines  to understand how this happens first you  need to understand how power gets to  your house  power plants generate electricity the  voltage is cranked up at a substation  and sent across long distance  transmission lines  crank down again then sent through  distribution lines that connect to your  home  in many cases everything from the plant  to the power poles are owned by a  regulated utility company  they supply you with the power and bill  you for how much you use  government officials let the utilities  charge enough to cover costs  and make a modest profit but high  electricity prices cost regulators to  re-examine this model  the big question um that drove a lot of  the thinking  in the 1980s around electricity and  and deregulation or restructuring was  did the whole system have to be a  monopoly or could there be  pieces of the electricity ecosystem  parts of how electricity is  generated moved over wires and then  supplied ultimately to customers  that could be competitive businesses and  kind of broken off  deregulation was in vogue from railroads  to telecommunications and the energy  market was no exception  it opened up to allow for companies to  compete to sell power to homes and  businesses  these new entities known as retail  energy companies  would buy electricity from generators  and sell it to consumers  usually over the local utilities wires  the argument for deregulation went that  giving consumers a choice between their  old utilities and new rivals would  create competitive pricing  and lower their bills eventually at  least 18 states deregulated retail  residential electricity to varying  degrees  but in many of them the promise savings  haven't materialized  according to a wall street journal  analysis between 2010 and 2019  u.s consumers who signed up with retail  energy companies paid 19.2 billion  dollars more  than they would have if they stuck with  their incumbent utilities  retail customers have paid the most in  texas which deregulated its energy  market the furthest  this system came under intense scrutiny  after a february 2021 winter storm  crippled its power grid  and left many texas residents with  electric bills that sort into the  thousands of dollars  electricity deregulation has worked for  business federal data show it led to  substantial savings for commercial and  industrial power customers whose  large-scale electricity use  give them the incentive to shop around  and seek expert guidance  the retail power industry largely  languished until about a decade ago  when many states adopted a regulation  called purchase of receivables  that made the traditional utility  companies solely responsible for unpaid  bills  experts have said that because they were  no longer responsible for unpaid bills  retail power companies didn't have to  worry about the risk of signing up lower  income consumers  there are often many many  variations in the kind of electricity  contract that you might sign with a  retail provider  just as for cell phone contracts you  might be negotiating about data  and minutes and you know  other bells and whistles that might come  with a contract that might increase its  attractiveness  a second really important similarity  has to do with the sort of central role  that  getting customers plays in the business  model of retail electricity  providers experts say retail energy  sales people often go door to door and  offer perks for switching to their  service such as gift cards or  free light bulbs many states have  investigated retail providers marketing  tactics that target elderly and poor  customers  as well as heavily minority  neighborhoods where electricity bills  account for larger shares of income  a 2018 illinois lawsuit said an energy  retailer carried out a particularly  egregious marketing scheme  that disproportionately impacted  african-american consumers on the south  and west side of chicago  and an analysis of the connecticut  retail power industry revealed that  retail power customers receiving state  assistance to cover electricity costs  known as hardship customers paid  premiums that were on average nearly 50  percent higher than other customers for  retail providers  susan baldwin an independent consultant  for public utilities and consumer  advocates  says some retail companies have  questionable marketing tactics with  aggressive sales pitches aimed at  enrolling customers  it shows disproportionate harm to those  who can least afford to pay for an  essential service this is not a luxury  item this is electricity  part of the problem is that many  consumers don't know who is selling them  their power  in most states that allow competitive  electricity markets for residential  customers  bills are provided by the incumbent  utility company even if the power is  delivered by a retail supplier  the identity of the retailer is  disclosed somewhere on the bill  but it isn't always obvious here's one  way to check if you're paying more for  power  look for the part of the bill that  details price comparison information  there you should find the price for the  so-called standard offer service  or sos for one kilowatt hour of  electricity  that is the average rate charged by the  incumbent utility  next look for how much your electric  supplier charged for that billing period  which is often reported alongside the  name of the company  take the rate your electric supplier  charged and multiply it by the number of  kilowatt hours used  to get the amount paid to the retail  supplier  to figure out the difference between  what you paid to the supplier  and what you would have paid to your  utility multiply the sos rate by the  kilowatt hours used and subtract that  amount from the amount paid to the  supplier  big retail companies can see that there  has been some bad behavior in the market  and that the industry needs to improve  its reputation  and industry backers say residential  customers need better education from the  industry and regulators  on how the retail electricity market  works and how it can benefit them  consumers can take advantage of the  deregulated marketplace by strategically  shifting to suppliers with the lowest  prices  but that can require reading their  contracts fine print  consumer advocates also encourage  customers to scrutinize their bill  and report any potential wrongdoing and  consumers can always switch back to  their incumbent utility provider  if you look at your bill and it seems to  be through the rule  and it's not what you were led to  believe you were told one thing  about rates being wonderfully low and  they end up being  terribly high call your  public utility commission and complain  retail power was supposed to be a light  bulb moment for the energy industry  but the cost of keeping that light bulb  on appeared to be higher than  they could be
 

